ʻao (8.8 km2/3.4 sq.","wpu":"Moʻorea-Maiʻao","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/Mooreamaiao.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Mouaputa","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-17.526293,"lo":-149.803276,"d":4646,"ds":"Mouaputa (alternately spelled Mouʼaputa) is a prominent peak on the eastern side of the island of Moorea in French Polynesia. It lies about 3 kilometres (1.9 mi) southeast of the head of Cook's Bay and about 3 kilometres (1.9 mi) northeast of the highest point in Moorea, Mount Tohivea.","wpu":"Mouaputa","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Mouaputa_and_Tohivea_from_Cooks_Bay%2C_2025.jpg/960px-Mouaputa_and_Tohivea_from_Cooks_Bay%2C_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Papetoai","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-17.495556,"lo":-149.872778,"d":5104,"ds":"Papetō'ai is an associated commune on the island of Mo'orea, in French Polynesia. It is part of the commune Moorea-Maiao. According to the 2022 census, it had a population of 2,214.","wpu":"Papetoai","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Moorea_ISS006.jpg/960px-Moorea_ISS006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Moʼorea","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-17.535,"lo":-149.835,"d":5114,"ds":"Moʼorea (English: or ; Tahitian: Moʼoreʼa, [moʔore(ʔ)a]), also spelled Moorea, is a volcanic island in French Polynesia lying 17 kilometres (11 mi) northwest of Tahiti. Moʼorea and Tahiti are the largest members of the Windward Islands, a group that is part of the larger Society Islands archipelago. Moʼoreʼa means 'yellow lizard' in Tahitian: Moʼo = lizard; Reʼa (from reʼareʼa) = yellow.","wpu":"Moʼorea","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/DSC00042_Polyn%C3%A9sia_Moor%C3%A9a_Island_Motu_Mo%C3%A9a_Lagoon_and_transportation_Boat_%288076082190%29.jpg/960px-DSC00042_Polyn%C3%A9sia_Moor%C3%A9a_Island_Motu_Mo%C3%A9a_Lagoon_and_transportation_Boat_%288076082190%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Teavaro","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-17.507778,"lo":-149.77,"d":6158,"ds":"Te'avaro is an associated commune on the island of Mo'orea, in French Polynesia. It is part of the commune Moʻorea-Maiʻao. According to the 2022 census, it had a population of 2,585.","wpu":"Teavaro","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Moorea_ISS006.jpg/960px-Moorea_ISS006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mouaroa","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-17.544636,"lo":-149.848894,"d":6582,"ds":"Mouaroa (alternately spelled Mouʼaroa or Mouʼa Roa) is a prominent peak near the center of Moorea, an island in French Polynesia in the central Pacific Ocean. Described as being \"shark-tooth-shaped\", Mouaroa stands about 3 kilometres (1.9 mi) south of the head of Opunohu Bay and about 2 kilometres (1.2 mi) west of the highest point in Moorea, Mount Tohivea.","wpu":"Mouaroa","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Mouaroa%2C_French_Polynesia%2C_December%2C_2011.jpg/960px-Mouaroa%2C_French_Polynesia%2C_December%2C_2011.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mount Tohivea","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-17.550912,"lo":-149.822194,"d":6779,"ds":"Mount Tohivea (or Tohiea) is the highest point on the island of Mo'orea in French Polynesia at 1,207 metres (3,960 ft). The mountain is about 3.2 kilometres (2.0 mi) south of the settlement of Pao Pao, and is easily visible from the west coast of nearby Tahiti, approximately 24 kilometres (15 mi) to the east.","wpu":"Mount Tohivea","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Tohiea_lagon.jpg/960px-Tohiea_lagon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Afareaitu","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-17.549444,"lo":-149.790556,"d":7551,"ds":"ʻĀfareaitu is an associated commune on the island of Moʻorea, in French Polynesia. It is part of the commune Moʻorea-Maiʻao. According to the census in 2022, it had a population of 4,055.","wpu":"Afareaitu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Moorea_ISS006.jpg/960px-Moorea_ISS006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Air Moorea Flight 1121","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-17.501667,"lo":-149.746111,"d":8466,"ds":"Air Moorea Flight 1121 was a de Havilland Canada DHC-6 Twin Otter which crashed into the ocean shortly after takeoff from Moorea Airport on Moorea Island in French Polynesia on 9 August 2007 killing all 20 people on board. It was bound for Tahiti's Faaʻa International Airport on a regular 7-minute service, one of the shortest in the world, scheduled 40 times a day.","wpu":"Air Moorea Flight 1121","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Air_Moorea_DHC-6_Twin_Otter_%28_F-OIQI%29_on_final_approach_into_Papeete.jpg/960px-Air_Moorea_DHC-6_Twin_Otter_%28_F-OIQI%29_on_final_approach_into_Papeete.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Air Moorea Flight 1121 was a de Havilland Canada DHC-6 Twin Otter which crashed into the ocean shortly after takeoff from Moorea Airport on Moorea Island in French Polynesia on 9 August 2007 killing all 20 people on board. It was bound for Tahiti's Faaʻa International Airport on a regular 7-minute service, one of the shortest in the world, scheduled 40 times a day. The crash resulted from loss of control due to failure of the airplane's elevator cable.","wpu":"Air Moorea Flight 1121","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Air_Moorea_DHC-6_Twin_Otter_%28_F-OIQI%29_on_final_approach_into_Papeete.jpg/960px-Air_Moorea_DHC-6_Twin_Otter_%28_F-OIQI%29_on_final_approach_into_Papeete.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}